1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:04,100
You click a checkbox labeled I'm not
a robot, and the page lets you in.

2
00:00:04,860 --> 00:00:07,040
It happens hundreds of
millions of times a day.

3
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:12,960
Every sign-up form, every login, every
ticket queue, you click it without thinking.

4
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:15,420
But the test was never the checkbox.

5
00:00:16,180 --> 00:00:19,300
In the seconds before you clicked,
your browser was being measured.

6
00:00:19,840 --> 00:00:24,640
The path your cursor took across the page,
the exact millisecond between arriving

7
00:00:24,680 --> 00:00:29,270
and reaching for the box, the cookies
you carry, the way your screen rendered

8
00:00:29,360 --> 00:00:33,800
pixels. By the time you got to the
checkbox, the verdict was already in.

9
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:35,800
The click was just the receipt.

10
00:00:36,340 --> 00:00:39,980
Without that measurement, every sign-up
form on the Internet would drown in

11
00:00:40,060 --> 00:00:42,460
automated traffic within minutes.

12
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:46,900
By industry estimates, bad bots account
for nearly a third of all web traffic.

13
00:00:47,330 --> 00:00:52,080
They scrape databases, generate spam by
the millions, and try stolen passwords

14
00:00:52,120 --> 00:00:54,730
against login pages
every hour of every day.

15
00:00:55,420 --> 00:01:00,260
The free email account, the concert ticket,
even the comment box under this video,

16
00:01:00,920 --> 00:01:02,200
every one of them is a target.

17
00:01:02,560 --> 00:01:06,760
Today we are going to look at why telling
humans from computers got harder, not

18
00:01:06,820 --> 00:01:12,200
easier. How three generations of CAPTCHAs
were each broken in turn, and why the test

19
00:01:12,280 --> 00:01:15,560
you passed had nothing to do
with the checkbox you clicked.

20
00:01:15,620 --> 00:01:18,040
Let's start with the Turing test.

21
00:01:18,080 --> 00:01:21,320
In nineteen fifty, Alan Turing
proposed a simple game.

22
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:24,980
A human judge talks to two
strangers through a keyboard.

23
00:01:25,520 --> 00:01:28,440
One is a person, one is a program.

24
00:01:28,480 --> 00:01:31,650
If the judge can't tell which is
which, the program has passed.

25
00:01:32,320 --> 00:01:34,880
A CAPTCHA is the same test run backwards.

26
00:01:35,480 --> 00:01:37,080
The judge is the computer.

27
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:39,240
The strangers are
everybody trying to log in.

28
00:01:39,940 --> 00:01:45,320
The acronym spells it out: completely
automated public Turing test to tell computers

29
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:50,420
and humans apart. It was coined in two
thousand and three by a researcher named Luis

30
00:01:50,460 --> 00:01:54,080
von Ahn, the same person
who later built Duolingo.

31
00:01:54,880 --> 00:01:59,780
The whole game rests on one assumption:
There exists some task that is easy for

32
00:01:59,790 --> 00:02:01,400
humans and hard for machines.

33
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:05,820
Find that task, wrap it in a
checkbox, and bots stay out.

34
00:02:06,580 --> 00:02:09,500
For twenty years, that assumption
held, until it didn't.

35
00:02:10,220 --> 00:02:14,020
In nineteen ninety-seven, two teams
independently invented the same trick.

36
00:02:14,440 --> 00:02:17,420
Show the user a smudged word
and ask them to type it back.

37
00:02:18,220 --> 00:02:20,480
Computers couldn't read distorted images.

38
00:02:20,840 --> 00:02:26,240
Humans could. By two thousand and one, PayPal
was using CAPTCHAs to block fraudulent

39
00:02:26,340 --> 00:02:31,500
sign-ups. Soon every major site had a
small twisted image gating the front door.

40
00:02:32,300 --> 00:02:36,680
The trick worked because reading distorted
text required three skills at once:

41
00:02:37,320 --> 00:02:42,740
recognizing letters, no matter how warped,
separating ones that overlap, and passing

42
00:02:42,780 --> 00:02:44,180
the whole word into meaning.

43
00:02:44,900 --> 00:02:47,440
Trivial for a human, a
nightmare for a machine.

44
00:02:47,860 --> 00:02:49,700
The wall held for almost a decade.

45
00:02:50,250 --> 00:02:54,540
Then it didn't. In late two thousand and
nine, just after Google had acquired a

46
00:02:54,600 --> 00:02:59,350
system called reCAPTCHA, a researcher
named Jonathan Wilkins published a paper

47
00:02:59,500 --> 00:03:02,500
showing he could solve it
eighteen percent of the time.

48
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:07,010
The next summer, a hacker named Chad Houck
stood up at DEF CON and pushed the number

49
00:03:07,040 --> 00:03:08,240
to one in three.

50
00:03:08,660 --> 00:03:12,380
By two thousand and eighteen, a single
research paper had broken eleven of the

51
00:03:12,400 --> 00:03:17,260
distorted text schemes still in use across
thirty-two of the top fifty websites,

52
00:03:17,580 --> 00:03:20,460
including Microsoft, Wikipedia, and eBay.

53
00:03:21,130 --> 00:03:23,060
The bots could read again.

54
00:03:23,120 --> 00:03:26,980
By two thousand and fourteen,
Google was ready with version two.

55
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:28,740
It turned the problem inside out.

56
00:03:29,420 --> 00:03:34,020
Instead of reading characters, you
recognized objects, a three-by-three grid of

57
00:03:34,040 --> 00:03:37,720
photographs. Select all
squares with crosswalks.

58
00:03:38,320 --> 00:03:40,180
Select all squares with traffic lights.

59
00:03:40,600 --> 00:03:42,640
Select all squares with fire hydrants.

60
00:03:43,320 --> 00:03:44,520
There was a second motive.

61
00:03:45,100 --> 00:03:49,600
Every click trained Google's image
recognition on real photographs, labeling

62
00:03:49,620 --> 00:03:54,340
crosswalks, lights, and signs that
fed into the self-driving car project.

63
00:03:54,480 --> 00:03:58,680
Billions of free training images, courtesy
of everyone trying to log into Reddit.

64
00:03:59,510 --> 00:04:02,329
The wall against bots was
also a labor pipeline.

65
00:04:02,980 --> 00:04:05,100
For about a decade, this worked.

66
00:04:05,140 --> 00:04:10,420
Then it stopped. In twenty twenty-four,
researchers at ETH Zurich fine-tuned an

67
00:04:10,540 --> 00:04:14,840
open-source object detection model
called YOLO on fourteen thousand traffic

68
00:04:14,920 --> 00:04:20,340
photographs. Per image, accuracy ranged
from sixty-nine percent on motorcycles to

69
00:04:20,380 --> 00:04:22,160
one hundred percent on fire hydrants.

70
00:04:22,760 --> 00:04:27,620
But reCAPTCHA lets you retry, so across
an average session, the bot passed every

71
00:04:27,720 --> 00:04:31,180
single test. Earlier attacks had
topped out at seventy percent.

72
00:04:31,780 --> 00:04:33,380
The wall fell, again.

73
00:04:34,120 --> 00:04:36,540
The YOLO researchers
exposed a deeper problem.

74
00:04:37,020 --> 00:04:39,580
Solving the puzzle wasn't
enough on its own.

75
00:04:39,640 --> 00:04:44,480
To pass, their bot also had to fake cookies,
route through a VPN, and simulate human

76
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:48,540
mouse movements. The visible
test had stopped being the test.

77
00:04:49,120 --> 00:04:52,860
So in two thousand and seventeen, Google
released reCAPTCHA version three.

78
00:04:53,580 --> 00:04:55,880
No more crosswalks, no more checkboxes.

79
00:04:56,600 --> 00:05:00,920
The system runs invisibly in the background
and scores every visitor from zero to

80
00:05:01,020 --> 00:05:06,380
one, bot to human, based on how they move,
how they type, and what they looked like

81
00:05:06,420 --> 00:05:12,260
before they arrived. hCAPTCHA and Cloudflare
Turnstile work the same way, but AI

82
00:05:12,380 --> 00:05:17,020
mimics now. Generative models
paint believable mouse paths.

83
00:05:17,060 --> 00:05:21,270
Headless browsers spoof every
fingerprint signal V3 once trusted.

84
00:05:21,920 --> 00:05:26,940
And in twenty twenty-three, during a red
team study of GPT-4, the model hired a

85
00:05:26,980 --> 00:05:31,640
worker on Taskrabbit, claimed to have a
vision impairment, and asked them to solve

86
00:05:31,680 --> 00:05:33,080
the CAPTCHA on its behalf.

87
00:05:33,780 --> 00:05:36,600
They did. The bot didn't beat the test.

88
00:05:36,640 --> 00:05:37,940
It paid a human to take it.

89
00:05:38,720 --> 00:05:43,850
Every checkbox you click, every grid of
crosswalks you sort, every hesitation your

90
00:05:43,880 --> 00:05:48,180
cursor makes on its way to the login
button, somewhere a system is watching,

91
00:05:48,390 --> 00:05:53,130
scoring, and quietly conceding that the
gap between human and machine is closing

92
00:05:53,140 --> 00:05:55,420
faster than the test can keep up.

93
00:05:55,480 --> 00:05:58,120
The next time you click,
I'm not a robot, remember.

94
00:05:59,280 --> 00:06:01,020
The box doesn't know
whether you are human.

95
00:06:01,620 --> 00:06:06,020
The system behind it watched how you got
there, how fast you clicked, what cookies

96
00:06:06,060 --> 00:06:09,180
you carried, and what the page
rendered like under your cursor.

97
00:06:09,810 --> 00:06:11,500
The checkbox is the punctuation.

98
00:06:12,080 --> 00:06:13,400
The sentence was already written.

99
00:06:14,020 --> 00:06:18,620
Researchers estimate we've spent eight
hundred and nineteen million hours, over six

100
00:06:18,780 --> 00:06:23,600
billion dollars in wages, clicking crosswalks
and checkboxes to prove we are human.

101
00:06:25,310 --> 00:06:27,300
The bots have already
learned how to pretend.
